Abstract
Perovskite solar cells (PSCs) had received great attention as a result of their recent rapid increasing efficiency. However, the stability of PSCs is still a challenge due to the degradation of the perovskite layer caused by the high-energy ultraviolet (UV) irradiation. Inspired by the luminescent down converting ability for UV blocking and conversion as well as energy transfer between suitable rare earth (RE) ions, a planar $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ perovskite solar cell using $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ and Eu3+ co-doped $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ electron transfer layer was designed. By optimizing the $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}\mathrm{and~Eu}^{3+}$ doping concentration, the REs co-doped $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL combines the advantages of high electron extraction and lower interfacial recombination caused by REs introduction, a power conversion efficiency of $19.01\%$ was obtained. In addition, benefit from the enhanced ability to convert UV light into visible light of the co-doped ETL, the PSCs can sustain higher than at least $80\%$ of the original efficiency over 25 days of full sunlight irradiation or after $100\mathrm{~h~}$ of UV illumination. Moreover, since the low-temperature pulsed laser deposition was adopted in ETL fabrication process, the large area $(225\ \mathrm{mm}^{2})$ ) and flexibility devices were further explored, with PCEs of $12.60\%$ and $15.48\%$ , respectively. This work indicates that $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ and ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ co-doped ETLs are effective and promising method to enhance the photovoltaic performance and UV stability of PSCs, which can be further applied in other PSCs with different ETLs and co-doping types.
Keywords: Perovskite solar cells, Rare earth doped, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , Pulsed laser deposition, Stability, Flexibility.
## 1. Introduction
Organic-inorganic hybrid perovskite solar cells (PSCs) has experienced unprecedented improvement in a few short years, it shows great potential for commercialization and outdoor applications. Up to now, the champion power conversion efficiency (PCE) of $22.7\%$ has obtained in the PSCs by employing conventional n- i-p structures with intensive research[1-6]. $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , as the most common electron transport layer (ETL) material, has been broadly adopted in PSCs in relation to its excellent transparency, carrier separation ability, environmental stability, and controllability[7]. It plays an important role in promoting electron collection and transportation from the light absorbing layer to the corresponding electrodes[8]. However, the incompletely matched band alignment with perovskite layer and the limited conductivity of pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ lead to the accumulation of electrons in the interface, which consequently limits the PCE and stability of the PSCs[9, 10]. In addition, the high-temperature annealing process in the mesoporous structure also restricts its further application in the flexible device.
Metal doping has been considered to be an effective way to mitigate the above-mentioned issues. A number of efforts have revealed that metal doping has a major effect on adjusting the band structure and trap states of semiconductoroxide, which in turn affects the carrier transport, recombination and collection in devices[11]. Among the different metal dopants, rare earth (RE) elements possess abundant energy level which originate from their special electron/photon transitions between 4f-5d orbits[12].  Those transitions are sensitive to the structures and composition of the substrate. As a result, RE doping has been extensively applied for modification of various semiconductor oxides and improved optical and electrical properties have been achieved[13-15]. For example, Gao et.al (2016) successfully enhanced the properties of PSC device by doping ${\mathrm{La}}^{3+}$ into the mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer, with an increasing Fermi level. Finally, improvement of $27\%$ in PCE had been achieved $(15.42\%)$ when compared to that of pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer $(12.11\%$ )[16]. Roose et al. (2016) obtained a high-quality $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL via $\mathrm{Nd}^{3+}$ doping in order to improve electron transport and reducing charge recombination in the PSCs, a results which indicates that a very low Nd concentration $(0.3\%)$ could enable the PSCs to have a stabilized PCE of $>18\%$ [17]. In addition, Jiang et.al (2017) employed a mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer with attached ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ which improved the UV light utilization via the down-conversion ability of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ : ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ nanocrystals and an improvement of $29.2\%$ in PCE was obtained.[18]. However, the present works regarding the PSCs using RE doping $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layers mainly focused on the single dopant. As already proved, co-doping is more beneficial to obtain an enhanced photovoltaic performance by means of compensating the negative effects of single doping or further enlarged the regulating range of carrier mobility, Fermi level etc[19-21]. In addition, in the case of co-doping of REs, energy transfer process can be effectively sensitized through reasonable designing of the rare earth co-doping system[22]. This phenomenon will be very attractive in enhancing the optical conversion ability and thus improve the solar spectrum utilization. Up to now, as far as we know, there has been no study focusing on the REs co-doping system in PSCs.
Accordingly, in this work, $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ and ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ co-doped $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\colon\mathrm{Sm}^{3+},~\mathrm{Eu}^{3+})$ ETLs are explored and applied in n-i-p PSCs, whereas ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions was applied as the down-conversion dopant to increase the UV light utilization and also protect the degradation of perovskite layer from UV light, $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ ions dopant was used as the sensitizer to enhanced the down-conversion ability of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions by broadening the excitation wavelength range for ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ emission due to the energy transfer from $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ to $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}[23]$ . PLD technique is adopted as the film deposition technique, which was further used for low-temperature, large area and flexible films preparation. The optimized devices exhibit an enhanced PCE of $19.01\%$ , highly improved light and UV stability $80\%$ of initial PCE remains after 100 h). In addition, large area device (225 $\mathrm{mm}^{2}$ ) and flexible devices are also fabricated which show PCE of $12.60\%$ and $15.48\%$ , respectively.
## 2 Experiment section
## 2.1 Materials synthesis
Lead iodide (PbI2), dimethylsulfoxide (DMSO), chlorobenzol (CB), dimethylformamide (DMF), and tetrabutyl titanate were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich. 2,2',7,7'-Tetrakis[N,N-di(4-methoxyphenyl)amino]-9,9'-spirobifluorene (Spiro-OMeTAD) was purchased from Xi’an Polymer Light Technology Corp. Europium (III) chloride hexahydrate $\mathrm{(EuCl_{3}.6H_{2}O)}$ ), Samarium chloride hexahydrate $\mathrm{(SmCl_{3}.6H_{2}O)}$ ) were purchased from Ruike National Engineering Research Center for
Rare Earth Metallurgical and Functional Materials. All other materials were purchased from Macklin. All the chemicals were used as received without any further purification.
In this work, methylammonium Iodide (MAI) was synthesized by mixing 27 mL of methylamine $40\mathrm{\mt}\%$ in absolute ethanol) and $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of hydroiodic acid (57 wt% in aqueous solution) in $250~\mathrm{mL}$ brown round bottom flask at $0^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ for 4 h with intense stirring. Afterward, the solution was transferred to a rotary evaporator at a constant temperature of $60^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize4h}$ to obtain a white powder. The powder was repeatedly dissolved in absolute ethanol and crystallized in anhydrous ether for 3 times. The final MAI powder was dried at $60^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ in a vacuum drying oven for $12{\mathrm{~h~}}$ High-quality $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ : $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ , ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ films were prepared by PLD technology. REs doped $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ powder was synthesized by an improved sol-gel method. In a typical process, 10 mmol tetrabutyl titanate was added into a mixed solution composed of $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of absolute ethanol, 1 mL of acetic acid, and 1 mL of hydrochloric acid mixed solution, and stirred for $6\mathrm{~h~}$ at room temperature. Hereafter, $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of $0.1\ \mathrm{\mM\EuCl_{3}.6H_{2}O}$ and $\mathrm{SmCl_{3}}.6\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ (in absolute ethanol) was poured into the above solution. The mixture was continuously stirred for $6\textup{h}$ to acquire a clear and transparent liquid. Then, the final solution was vacuum dried to powder in $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $6\mathrm{~h~}$ followed by annealing in air at $500^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ for $5\mathrm{h}$ Finally, the target for PLD deposition was prepared by cold pressing $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ and ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ co-doped $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ powder into a columniform target with a diameter of $1.2\ \mathrm{cm}$ and a thickness of $0.1\ \mathrm{cm}$ which was sintered at $1000^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ for $10\mathrm{~h~}$ in air. For comparing the ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ doped $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\colon\mathrm{Eu}^{3+})$ and pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , PLD targets were also prepared
following similar preparation processes.
## 2.2 Device Fabrication
Fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO) glass substrates were etched by zinc powder together with $^{2\mathbf{M}}$ of HCl, and they were successively ultra-sonicated in acetone, isopropanol, deionized water, and ethanol for $20~\mathrm{min}$ , respectively, then dried in air. Before using, the FTO substrates were treated under oxygen plasma for 10 min to remove the surface organic residues. A compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer was spin coated $0.15\textbf{M}$ titanium diisopropoxide bis-(acetylacetonate) (75 wt $\%$ in isopropanol) in 1-butanol solution onto the cleaned FTO glass at 2000 rpm for 30 s and sintered at $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . Thereafter, the different $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs were deposited via PLD method. During PLD deposition process, the PLD chamber was first evacuated to about $\mathsf{4}\times\mathsf{10}^{-4}$ Pa. Then, the deposition chamber was evacuated using a molecular pump to a base pressure of ${{1.5}{\times}{10}^{-5}}$ Pa. Pulsed UV KrF $\lambda=248\ \mathrm{nm}$ $6\:\mathrm{Hz}$ repetition rate) exciter laser with an energy density of $2~\mathrm{J.cm}^{-2}$ was used for ablation. The distance between the target and the Fluorine doped tin oxide (FTO) or PET substrates was kept at $8\ \mathrm{cm}$ and the deposited time was fixed at about $50\ \mathrm{min}$ . Finally, different dense $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs was obtained. For the perovskite layer, the precursor solution was prepared by dissolving $0.1589\ \mathrm{g}$ of $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ (MAI) and $0.4610\ \mathrm{g}$ of $\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{2}}$ (molar ratio of 1:1) in $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of mixed solutions of DMF and DMSO with the volume ratio of 7:3. The MAPbI3 solution was spin-coated on the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs with $1000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $10\mathrm{~s~}$ and $4000\ \mathrm{rpm}$ for 30 s, respectively. In this process, $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of chlorobenzene was dropped on the spinning films after 15 s of the second spinning program. Then, the substrates were annealed at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $5\ \mathrm{min}$ in a nitrogen-protected glove box. Subsequently, the Spiro-OMeTAD solution $\mathrm{50~mg}$ of Spiro-MeOTAD, $22.5~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of 4-tert-butylpyridine, and $22.5\ \mathrm{\upmuL}$ of acetonitrile solution containing 170 mg.mL-1 of lithium bis-(trifluoromethylsulfonyl)imide in $1\ \mathrm{mL}$ of chlorobenzene) was deposited on the surface of MAPbI3 layer by spin-coating at $1500~\mathrm{rpm}$ for 30 s in a nitrogen atmosphere. Finally, the Au electrode with a thickness of 100 nm was deposited by thermal evaporation in a vacuum environment $(5\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{Pa}).$
## 2.3 Device Characterization
The surface morphology of the films and the cross-sectional view of the device were characterized by a SIRION field emission scanning electron microscope (SEM). The energy-dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) spectra were recorded on a Nova_NanoSEM430 instrument. X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns for $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ films were carried out on a Rigaku D/max 2550 X-ray diffractometer, using a monochromatized Cu target radiation source at a scanning rate of $4\%\mathrm{{inin}}$ . The roughness of different doped $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ films was characterized by atomic force microscopy (AFM, 5500, Agilent, Santa Clara, CA). Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was executed by a CHI630E electrochemical analyzer (ChenHua, China). The absorption spectra were recorded by using a UV-1800 spectrometer (Shimadzu). The photoluminescence (PL) characterization was carried out by using a fluorescence luminescence spectrometer. The ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) spectra were recorded by using a Thermo ESCALAB 250 (Thermo Fisher Scientific). The current-voltage (I-V) characteristics were measured under 1 Sun condition $\mathrm{100~mW~cm^{-2}}$ and $\mathbf{AM}1.5\mathrm{~G~}$ irradiation) using an ABET Sun 2000 solar simulator system, and Keithley model 2400 was employed as a digital source meter in ambient conditions. The light intensity was calibrated with a reference silicon cell (RERA Solutions RR-1002). Incident photo-to-current conversion efficiency (IPCE) spectra was recorded using a SolarCellScan100 system from 400 to 850 nm (ZOLIX). The co-doped $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ thin films were grown on FTO or PET substrates using the PLD system which consists of Neocera PIONEER 180 deposition chamber and the MPEX PRO 205 pulsed laser.
## 3. Results and discussion
3.1 Structure and morphology
The device architecture of synthesized inverted planar PSCs on FTO is shown in Figure 1a, constructed from the structure of $\mathrm{FTO}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ : Sm3+, Eu3+/MAPbI3/spiro-OMeTAD/Au. Figure 1b shows the energy levels of each functional layer in the inverted planar PSCs, where the energy level of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\colon\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ , ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ was calculated from the UPS spectra and the UV spectra (Fig. S1 a and b) and those of $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ , spiro-OMeTAD, and Au are cited from literature [24]. The Fermi levels of the pristine and the co-doped $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ films was calculated to be $-4.69$ and −4.11 eV from the UPS spectra, respectively. As calculated, the REs doping effectively increase the Fermi level of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ to $0.58\ \mathrm{eV.}$ This phenomenon can be attributed to the REs ions having higher binding affinity than oxygen ions, leading to scavenging of oxygen ions and the introduction of vacancies on the surface of ETLs when $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ and ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ co-doped into $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}[16]$ . The increase of the Fermi energy has been proved to be favorable for improving the carrier injection from perovskite to the ETL, this process can further result in higher $\mathrm{v_{OC}}$ in planar perovskite solar cell.[25, 26] Besides, together with the UV spectra of pristine and different co-doped $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ films, the energy band positions are further determined. As shown in Figure 1b, the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\colon\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ , ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ETL shows a better band alignment compared to that of the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL, showing the electron can be transferred more easily from perovskite layer to $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\colon\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ , ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ETL[27]. The cross-sectional SEM image of a complete PSC device is exhibited in Figure 1c. As seen, every layer is uniformed deposited, and the thickness of the co-doped $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , MAPbI3, Spiro-OMeTAD, and Au layers are determined to be 40, 400, 200, and $100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , respectively.
To get more information on the influence of REs co-doping on $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ substrate, the ratios of $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}/\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ are controlled to be 1, 1.25, and 2, respectively, by fixing the doping amount of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ as $1\%$ and adjusting the concentration of $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ from $0.5\%$ to $1\%$ . Figure 1d shows the XRD patterns of different RE-doped $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ as well as the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layers. The XRD spectra of all the samples are exclusively indexed to the anatase phase of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (JCPDS No. 75-1573), which are well crystallized with the main diffraction peaks of (101), (004), and (200) crystal planes[28]. There is no trace fabricated via PLD method exhibits excellent crystallinity of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . The right part of Figure 1d exhibits the enlarged XRD patterns of (101) crystal planes around $25^{\circ}\ 2\uptheta$ degree. The (101) peaks gradually shift to lower degree, thereby increasing the doping amount of RE ions. It is known that the ionic radius of $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ and Eu3+ are 0.96 and 0.94 $\mathring\mathbf{A}$ , respectively, which are larger than that of $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}(0.61\textup{\AA})$ . In this case, RE ions may have difficulty to effectively replace the crystalline site of $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ due to the mismatched diameter of ions[29, 30]. However, previous studies have revealed that RE ions could occupy the surface sites adjacent to the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanocrystals in the amorphous form, which could further influence the valence bonds condition of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . In our case, the position of (101) crystal plane shifts gradually to the lower degree side, indicating that the corresponding lattices expand with the increasing amount of RE ions [18, 31]. The UV- vis absorption spectra were used to estimate the impact of the $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ and Eu3+ doping on the band gap of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ films. The extrapolated plots of (ahv)2 versus hv are shown in Figure S1b which is acquired from the absorption spectra. Compared to the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer, the introduction of $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ and ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ can effectively increase the band gap. The more the doping amounts of $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ , the larger the band gap. As exhibited in Figure S1c, different doping concentrations of $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ and ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ was able to adjust the band gap of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ from 2.87 to 2.97 eV[26]. This shift can be attributed to a charge transfer effect in the Ti-O-RE bond caused by the slight modifications to the density of the materials of states after incorporation of trivalent rare earth ions[32, 33].
The electron extraction behaviors of different doped $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ as well as pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ films are verified by performing the typical I-V curves. Figure 1e is the I-V curves measured under applied voltage from -2 to $2\mathrm{V}.$ The linear I-V relationship is observed, demonstrating the no Schottky contact was formed. All the RE-doped TiO2 films exhibit a higher conductivity than that of the pristine one, indicating the better capability of electron extraction of the RE-doped $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}[14]$ . Among the different doping concentrations, the conductivity of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\mathrm{:Eu}_{1\%}^{3+}$ film is the lowest and it is gradually increased with increase in the doping amount of $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ . The highest conductivity is obtained in ${\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}{\mathrm{:}}\thinspace{\mathrm{Sm}}_{1\%}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Eu}_{\%}^{3+}$ film, however its value only shows a slight increment when compared with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\colon\mathrm{Sm}_{0.8\%}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Eu}_{\%}^{3+}$ film. This indicates excess RE ions may act as scattering centers for charges and consequently hinder further increase of the conductivity[11]. It can be concluded that RE doping can reduce the defects density under the optimized concentration[24].
Herein, since $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ : $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Sm}}_{0.8\%}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Eu}_{\%}^{3+}$ , ETL exhibit the highest PCE as proved in the following photovoltaic performance test, more morphology and structure characterization is focused on it. Figure 2a shows the top-view SEM image of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ : $\mathrm{Sm}_{0.8\%}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Eu}_{\%}^{3+}$ layer deposited via PLD and a dense and homogeneous layer is observed. The EDS mapping of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\colon\mathrm{Sm}_{0.8\%}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Eu}_{\%}^{3+}$ layer demonstrates the coexistence of Ti, O, Sm, and Eu elements, the corresponding atomic percentages match well with the actual amount (Figure 2b). AFM image further confirms the high quality of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ : $\mathrm{Sm}_{0.8\%}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Eu}_{\%}^{3+}$ , the layer deposited via PLD method, in which a root mean square (RMS) value of $9.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ is obtained (Figure 2c). This value is lower than that of $10.4~\mathrm{nm}$ of single doped $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ : $\mathrm{Eu}_{\%}^{3+}$ film and $11.3~\mathrm{nm}$ of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ : $\ensuremath{\mathrm{S}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{m}}_{0.8\%}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Eu}_{\%}^{3+}$ film fabricated via spin-coating (Figure S2), demonstrating the advantage of the PLD technology in preparing high quality film compared with the conventional spin coating method.
Figure 3c shows the emission spectra of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ : $\ensuremath{\mathrm{S}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{m}}_{0.8\%}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Eu}_{\%}^{3+}$ and TiO2 :Eu31+% films that excited under different irradiation. As reported, $395~\mathrm{nm}$ and 405 nm are the typical excitation wavelength of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ ions, respectively. As the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\mathrm{:Eu}_{1\%}^{3+}$ film is excited by $395\ \mathrm{nm},$ , the well-known ${}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}\mathrm{\rightarrow}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{1}$ , $^5\mathrm{D}_{0}\rightarrow^{7}\mathrm{F}_{2}$ emission lines of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions can be found around 591 and $615~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , respectively. When the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\colon\thinspace\ensuremath{\mathbf{S}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{m}}_{3+\ 0.8\%}$ film is excited by $405\ \mathrm{nm}$ the characteristic transitions from $^4\mathrm{G}_{5/2}$ - $\cdot^{6}\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{J}/2}$ (J=5, 7, 9, and 11) of $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ ions are clearly observed. Moreover, when $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\colon\mathrm{Sm}_{0.8\%}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Eu}_{\%}^{3+}$ film is excited by $405\ \mathrm{nm}$ which is the typical excitation wavelength of $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ ion, only the ${}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}{\rightarrow}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{\mathrm{J}}$ $(\mathrm{J}{=}1,2)$ emission peaks of Eu3+ ions appear and no emission peaks related to $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ ions can be detected. In addition, the emission spectra intensity of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\colon\mathrm{Sm}_{3+}\ 0.8\%$ , $\mathrm{Eu}_{\%}^{3+}$ are enhanced at 615 nm compared to that of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\colon\mathrm{Eu}_{1\%}^{3+}$ , film. This phenomenon can be attributed to the efficient energy transfer between $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ and ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions. The possible energy transfer pathway from $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ to ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions is exhibited in Figure 2e. When the TiO2 : $\mathrm{Sm}_{0.8\%}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Eu}_{1\%}^{3+}$ film is excited by $405\ \mathrm{nm}$ corresponding to the ${}^{6}\mathrm{H}_{5/2}.{}^{4}\mathrm{K}_{11/2}$ of $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ , some electrons of the $^4{\bf K}_{11/2}$ level relax nonradiatively to $^4\mathrm{G}_{5/2}$ accompanied by phonons releasing. These electrons are then populated to ${}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}$ energy level of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions through resonance energy transfer without any red emission of $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ . However, when the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ : $\mathrm{Sm}_{0.8\%}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Eu}_{\%}^{3+}$ film is excited by $395\ \mathrm{nm}$ which is the typically excited wavelength of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions, only the ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ emission peaks can be detected, as presented in Figure 3c. This indicates that the energy transfer from ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ to $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ is invalid which can be assigned to the fact that the energy location of $^4\mathrm{G}_{5/2}$ level of $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ is higher than that of the ${}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}$ level of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ , which increases the difficulty of energy transfer from ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ to $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ . To prove this energy transfer process, the fluorescent decay curves were further investigated to the dynamical processes of different RE-doped TiO $\overline{{2}}$ films (Figure 2f). According to the fluorescent decay curves, the fluorescent lifetimes (τ) of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\colon\mathrm{Sm}_{0.8\%}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ : $\ensuremath{\mathrm{S}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{m}}_{0.8\%}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Eu}_{\%}^{3+}$ films are calculated to be 0.58 and $0.45~\mathrm{{\ms}}$ , respectively. As compared, the lifetime is shortened in TiO2: $\mathrm{Sm}_{0.8\%}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Eu}_{1\%}^{3+}$ film compared to the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\colon\mathrm{Sm}_{0.8\%}^{3+}$ film. This is evidence of the energy transfer from $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ to ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ , since the rate of nonradiative transition is faster than that of the radiative transition [34, 35]. It implies that the absorbed energy of $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ ions can be efficiently transferred to the ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions, demonstrating that $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ : $\mathrm{Sm}_{0.8\%}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Eu}_{1\%}^{3+}$ film possess stronger ability to utilize ultraviolet light and turn it into visible light.
To investigate the influence of REs doping as well as the fabrication method on the crystallinity of top perovskite layers, the morphologies of MAPbI3 perovskite layers on different ETLs are compared. As shown in Figure 3a-c, the crystal sizes continuously increase after RE introduction. In the case of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ : $\mathrm{Eu}_{\%}^{3+}$ ETL, the maximum grain size is around $150~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 3a). When further co-dope $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ ions into the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\colon\mathrm{Eu}_{1\%}^{3+}$ ETL, the maximum grain size is around $250~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 3b). The increased grain size of MAPbI3 is attributed to that RE ions and can improve the conductivity of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL as shown in the I-V curves in Figure 2b[36]. Moreover, the crystal size ( $\mathrm{100~nm},$ ) of MAPbI3 deposited on spin-coated $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ : $\ensuremath{\mathrm{S}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{m}}_{0.8\%}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Eu}_{\%}^{3+}$ ETL (Figure S3) is a little smaller than that deposited via PLD under the same doping condition. This result can be assigned to the reduced defects in PLD deposited $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL, which provides a uniform and dense substrate for the homogeneous growth of MAPbI3 (Figure 2a).
In addition, the charge extraction capacity of different $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs was elucidated by steady-state photoluminescence spectra with the MAPbI3 perovskite film on top of them. As shown in Figure 3d, an obvious PL quenching is observed after doping of $1\%$ ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ into the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ film at the excitation wavelength of 520 nm. Then, the degree of PL spectra become greater when $0.1\%$ $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ ions were further introduced, indicating that proper introduction of RE ions can enhance electron collection and transport efficiency of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs.
## 3.2 Device performance
Based on the above results, the PSCs were fabricated using different $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs. Figure 4a shows the J-V characteristics and the relevant parameters including PCE, short-circuit current $(\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{SC}})$ , open-circuit voltage $\mathrm{\DeltaV_{OC})}$ , and fill factor (FF) are compared and summarized in Figure 4b-e and Table S1 (measured from 18 devices and each device contained 5 test panels). As exhibited, the PSCs using RE-doped $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs present a better photovoltaic performance than that of pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . Very encouragingly, similar to the results of I-V curves, all the PSCs using co-doped $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs showed enhanced photovoltaic performance compared to the PSCs using the single ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ doped $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL. Especially, the device performance improves significantly as $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ : $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Sm}}_{0.8\%}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Eu}_{\%}^{3+}$ ETL is employed in PSC, and it exhibits the highest PCE of $19.01\%$ with a $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{J}}_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $22.47\mathrm{\mA.cm^{-2}}$ , a $\mathrm{v_{OC}}$ of $1.10~\mathrm{V},$ and a FF of $76.9\%$ . This PCE value is $8.2\%$ and $6.5\%$ higher compared to the devices using the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\mathrm{:Eu}_{1\%}^{3+}$ ETLs, respectively. Besides, the PCE of spin-coated TiO2: $\mathrm{Sm}_{0.8\%}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Eu}_{\%}^{3+}$ based PSCs is $16.90\%$ (Figure S4), much lower than the device prepared by the PLD method, and this result is consistent with previous studies[37, 38]. The steady-state current densities of PSC using $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\colon\mathrm{Sm}_{0.8\%}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Eu}_{\%}^{3+}$ ETL and the reference device are shown in Figure S5. The photocurrent density of PSC using $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\colon\mathrm{Sm}_{0.8\%}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Eu}_{\%}^{3+}$ ETL remains stable for at least 100 s, suggesting an efficient charge extraction ability due to the introduction of co-doped RE elements.
As compared in Figure 4b, the major improvement of PCE relates to the increased $\mathrm{\DeltaJ_{SC}}$ and FF, which can be attributed to the improved charge extraction and collection ability of the TiO2: $\mathrm{\DeltaSm}_{0.8\%}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Eu}_{\%}^{3+}$ ETL. As discussed above, the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\colon\ensuremath{\mathrm{S}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{m}}_{0.8\%}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Eu}_{\%}^{3+}$ , ETL shows a better band energy alignment to perovskite layer and enhanced electrical conductivity compared to that of pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL, all these advanced properties can validly lead to an increase of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{J}}_{\mathrm{SC}}$ and FF values [39-41]. To illustrate the reason for the increase of $\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{SC}}$ and FF in the REs doped device, the EIS spectra under dark condition were employed to explore the nonradiative recombination (Figure 4f). The used equivalent circuit model was provided in Figure S6, whereas ${\bf R}_{\mathrm{s}}$ is the interface resistance of the FTO, $\scriptstyle\mathrm{\mathsf{R}}_{\mathrm{reb}}$ and $\mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{reb}}$ are related to the interface resistance and capacitance of the TiO2/MAPbI3/Spiro-OMeTAD interface, respectively. Normally, a larger $\mathrm{\bfR}_{\mathrm{reb}}$ indicates a better ability of depressing the back-recombination from the conductive band of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ to spiro-MeOTAD, which is favorable to achieve a higher FF value[42]. Herein, the corresponding $\mathrm{\bfR}_{\mathrm{reb}}$ values are listed in Table S2. As compared, the $\mathrm{\bfR}_{\mathrm{reb}}$ value increases gradually with the doping concentration of REs, reaches the maximum value for the PSC using $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ : $\ensuremath{\mathrm{S}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{m}}_{0.8\%}^{3+}$ , Eu $\begin{array}{l}{{3+}}\\ {{1\%}}\end{array}$ $1\%$ ETL, and the $\mathrm{\bfR}_{\mathrm{reb}}$ value decreases with further increase in the doping amount, which agree with other results discussed above. Furthermore, the dark current densities between the PSCs using $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ : $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Sm}}_{0.8\%}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Eu}_{\%}^{3+}$ and pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs are evaluated and compared in Figure $4\mathrm{g}.$ . The $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\colon\mathrm{Sm}_{0.8\%}^{3+},\mathrm{Eu}_{1\%}^{3+}$ ETL based device shows small leakage current in the working bias range of 0–0.75 V. The current density increases at higher voltages (0.75-1.5V). This result demonstrates enhanced charge transportation and depressed recombination loss in a device using $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ and ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ co-doped TiO2[43, 44]. Figure 4h demonstrates the IPCE spectra of the PSC devices over the range from 400 to 820 nm. Consistent with the J-V curves, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\colon\mathrm{Sm}_{0.8\%}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Eu}_{\%}^{3+}$ device presents the highest IPCE over the whole studied range. It is worth mentioning that the IPCE curves showed an obvious increase between 400 to $440~\mathrm{nm}$ in the UV region than that of control device due to the cooperation of $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ and ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions, which can effectively increase the utilization of ultraviolet light and convert it into usable visible light.
Stability is an important factor that should be seriously considered for further commercial promotion since perovskite absorber is easy to degrade under illumination. Under ultraviolet irradiation, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL will extract electrons from $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{I}^{-}}$ at the interface between $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and MAPbI3 and deconstruct the structure of the perovskite[45].
Accordingly, the long-term stability of PSC using $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ : $\ensuremath{\mathrm{S}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{m}}_{0.8\%}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Eu}_{1\%}^{3+}$ ETL was investigated and compared with that of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\mathrm{:Eu}_{1\%}^{3+}$ as well as pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs.
Figure 5a shows the stability of the device under AM 1.5G simulated sunlight irradiation. The PCE of PSC using $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\colon\mathrm{Sm}_{0.8\%}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Eu}_{\%}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\mathrm{:Eu}_{1\%}^{3+}$ ETLs can well maintain more than $85\%$ and $73\%$ of the initial values after 25 days irradiation, respectively. While the PCE of the reference device using pristine $\mathbf{\hat{T}i O}_{2}$ quickly decreases to below $50\%$ of its original value. The stability effectively increase by $60\%$ after reasonable co-doping of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ ions into the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL. This significantly improved long-term stability of the as-proposed device can be attributed to the enhanced ‘UV block and transfer’ ability provided by the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\colon\mathrm{Sm}_{0.8\%}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Eu}_{\%}^{3+}$ ETL. Reasonable co-doped REs into the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer can efficiently transfer UV light into visible light when compared to $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ : $\mathrm{Eu}_{\%}^{3+}$ ETL as depicted in Figure 2d. To confirm the validity of the ‘UV block and transfer’ ability of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\colon\mathrm{Sm}_{0.8\%}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Eu}_{\%}^{3+}$ ETL, the long-term stability of the PSCs was further performed by exposing to UV light (365 nm) for 100 h. Note that $365~\mathrm{nm}$ irradiation is considered as the most realistic condition for the effect of UV aging on device stability[46]. As presented in Figure 5b, the PCE of the device using pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ quickly decreases down to $35\%$ of its original value after $100\mathrm{~h~}$ UV irradiation. With the help of ‘UV block and transfer’ ability from RE ions, the long-term UV stability was significantly maintained, which increased to $80\%$ and $70\%$ of the original values with the PSCs using $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\colon\mathrm{Sm}_{0.8\%}^{3+}$ , Eu $^{3+}_{1\%}$ and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\mathrm{:Eu}_{1\%}^{3+}$ ETLs, respectively. This demonstrates that ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ co-doped devices possess excellent UV blocking and optical conversion effects, even much more effective compared to single ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ doped device[47].
Interestingly, our method has the capability to fabricate large-area device with different active area up to $225~\mathrm{mm}^{2}$ . Figure 6c exhibits the photograph of different PSCs on the FTO substrate. The corresponding PCEs were measured and compared in Figure 5d. The average PCEs are $17.56\%$ , $16.61\%$ , $15.31\%$ , and $12.60\%$ for the PSCs with effective area of $2\times5$ , $5{\times}5$ , $10\times10$ and $15{\times}15\ \mathrm{mm}^{2}$ , respectively. As seen, the PCEs gradually decrease with increase in the effective area, because more defects may be produced in the larger area devices[48]. In addition, based on the good photovoltaic performance of the PSC using $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}{:}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{m}_{0.8\%}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Eu}_{\%}^{3+}$ , ETL together with the possibility for low temperature and large area preparation deposition of high quality films via PLD method, a flexible PSCs device were further constructed and investigated with the structure of PET/TiO2: $\ensuremath{\mathrm{S}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{m}}_{0.8\%}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Eu}_{1\%}^{3+}/\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}/$ spiro-OMeTAD/Au (Figure 5e and f). The J-V test of the flexible PSCs device ( $\mathrm{i}0\ \mathrm{mm}^{2}.$ ) in Figure 5e gives the highest PCE of $15.48\%$ with a $\mathrm{\DeltaJ_{SC}}$ of $21.66\mathrm{\mA.cm^{-2}}$ , a $\mathrm{v_{OC}}$ of $1.02{\mathrm{V}},$ and a FF of 71.70%, which is similar to the findings reported in previous literature[49].
## 4. Conclusions
On the basis of previous reports [16, 18, 50], $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ and ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ co-doped $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ were first employed as ETLs via low-temperature PLD process to improve the photovoltaic behavior of PSCs. By optimizing the co-doping concentration of REs, the optimized device exhibits a maximum PCE of $19.01\%$ with a $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{J}}_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $22.47\mathrm{\mA.cm^{-2}}$ , a $\mathrm{v_{OC}}$ of $1.10~\mathrm{V},$ , and a FF of $76.9\%$ . The obtained PCE improved $8.2\%$ in contrast with that of the control device $(17.56\%)$ , a feature similar to that observed in the single RE-doped system [18, 50]; moreover, due to the improved charge extraction and collection ability of the REs co-doped $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{ETL},}\end{array}$ , the PCE was even higher than those of the single RE-doped $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL in our case. Besides, this PCE was $12.5\%$ higher than the device using spin-coated REs co-doped $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL, broadly similar to previous studies [37, 38]. In addition, the effective energy transfer between $\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}$ and Eu3+ ions allows the suitable REs co-doping in $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs for preventing the decomposition of perovskite film caused by UV light. As a result, the photostability is highly enhanced over 25 days for full sunlight or $100\mathrm{~h~}$ of UV illumination. Moreover, the large area device $(225~\mathrm{mm}^{2})$ ) with a PCE of $12.60\%$ and flexible devices with a PCE of $15.48\%$ were also successfully achieved. This work provides a feasible method to improve the light stability and device performance of PSCs.
